      'The dispute between the branches associated with the titles Duke of Castro and Duke of Calabria centers on whether a change to the family\'s house law made around 1900, altering the line of succession among descendants, was ever validly promulgated and binding, a question of private dynastic law that has been litigated and argued in genealogical and legal circles for decades without ever being resolved by any state authority, since the kingdom itself vanished in 1861. This is a genuinely technical, ongoing dispute of the sort that occupies specialist journals on dynastic and nobility law rather than general historical accounts, and it parallels similar unresolved headship disputes in other extinct or dethroned royal houses where no reigning government exists to issue an authoritative ruling. Both branches maintain active dynastic orders (like the Constantinian Order of Saint George) and issue competing honors, keeping the dispute practically, if not politically, alive today. A related detail: a partial reconciliation agreement between the two branches was signed in 2014, though it did not fully settle the underlying succession question.',
