      'Leopold belonged to the Catholic Swabian Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen branch, related to the Protestant Prussian royal Hohenzollerns only as a distant cousin and entirely outside the Prussian line of succession, which is precisely why Bismarck saw his candidacy for Spain as a useful, relatively deniable way to pressure France without directly committing the Prussian crown itself, and why France\'s furious reaction, demanding guarantees the Hohenzollerns would never accept any such candidacy, still managed to escalate into full war despite Leopold\'s candidacy being formally withdrawn before fighting began. The subsequent Ems Dispatch affair, in which Bismarck edited a telegram account of Wilhelm I\'s meeting with the French ambassador to appear more insulting to France, is the direct proximate trigger for the war\'s declaration, but the underlying Hohenzollern candidacy crisis is what created the diplomatic opening Bismarck exploited. Distinguishing the Sigmaringen cadet branch clearly from the reigning Prussian line is essential to understanding why this whole affair was diplomatically deniable in the first place rather than an overt Prussian royal power grab. A related detail: Leopold\'s own son, later King Ferdinand I of Romania, shows how the same Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen branch went on to supply monarchs to an entirely different European throne just a few years after the failed Spanish bid.',
