      'Catherine was born Sophie Friederike Auguste of Anhalt-Zerbst, one of the numerous minor Anhalt principalities in the Holy Roman Empire, the daughter of a relatively obscure prince who nonetheless held a Prussian field marshal\'s rank, and her comparatively modest birth is part of why her later transformation into one of history\'s most consequential rulers is treated as such a striking case study in dynastic marriage strategy elevating minor houses into major thrones. This detail is genuinely obscure relative to general knowledge of Catherine the Great, since most popular accounts skip straight to her arrival in Russia and marriage to the future Peter III without dwelling on the specific, quite minor Anhalt-Zerbst origin, which had no independent political significance of its own by the eighteenth century. The Anhalt-Zerbst line itself went extinct in the male line not long after Catherine\'s era, its territory eventually absorbed into the other Anhalt principalities. A related detail: Catherine\'s mother, Johanna Elisabeth of Holstein-Gottorp, was distantly related to the Swedish and Russian ruling houses independently, meaning Catherine\'s eventual marriage into the Romanovs was not entirely without prior, if thin, genealogical connection.',
