      0,
      'Chambord, grandson of Charles X and the last direct descendant of the senior Bourbon line through Louis XV\'s grandsons, died childless in 1883, forcing French monarchists to either accept the Orléanist Comte de Paris (descended from Louis XV\'s line only via the more junior Orléans branch, disqualified in strict Legitimist eyes by Philippe Égalité\'s revolutionary sympathies) or look to the senior line\'s technical continuation through the Spanish Bourbons descending from Philip V, whose renunciations at Utrecht Legitimists had never fully accepted as binding on succession to the French throne specifically. This split, still nominally alive today between Orléanist and self-styled Legitimist claimants (currently centered on a Spanish-descended line), is treated by monarchist historians and genealogists as a live if practically inconsequential controversy, hinging on exactly the kind of Utrecht renunciation validity question that recurs across French succession law disputes since 1713. Chambord\'s own political caution, notably his refusal in 1873 to accept the tricolor flag as a condition for a parliamentary restoration, is often cited as having cost the Bourbons their last realistic chance at restoration even before his death settled the succession question. A related detail: the current senior Legitimist claimant traces descent through Alfonso XIII of Spain\'s line, making today\'s competing French pretenders both, somewhat ironically, more genealogically Spanish than French in immediate ancestry.',
