      'Wilhelmina was only ten years old when her father William III died in 1890, well below the Dutch constitutional age of majority for a reigning monarch, so her mother Emma, William\'s much younger second wife, served as regent under specific constitutional provision until Wilhelmina turned eighteen in 1898, a genuinely necessary legal mechanism rather than an honorary or customary arrangement. Emma\'s own competent and well-regarded regency is often credited by Dutch constitutional historians with stabilizing the monarchy during a vulnerable transition, especially given the succession crisis simultaneously unfolding over Luxembourg, which passed to Nassau-Weilburg specifically because Salic restrictions there (unlike in the Netherlands itself) barred Wilhelmina\'s succession. This distinguishes Emma\'s regency, a straightforward minority-age accommodation under Dutch law, from Salic-law disputes elsewhere in the family\'s holdings occurring in the very same years, two related but legally separate mechanisms that specialists must keep clearly apart. A related detail: Emma of Waldeck-Pyrmont came from another minor German principality, Waldeck-Pyrmont, whose comparatively obscure ruling house nonetheless produced not only a Dutch regent-consort but also a British-connected branch through a later Waldeck-Pyrmont marriage into the Hohenzollern family.',
